let's move on we got a big story to  start off the hour the crisis over the  shortage of baby formula this month the  out-of-stock rate hit an astounding 43  percent leaving many parents scrambling  relief could be in sight after the fda  cleared the way for the nation's leading  supplier to restart production at its  michigan plant still it could take up to  two months for that formula to actually  hit store shelves parents have been  telling us about their desperate search  for supplies in the meantime here's what  we've learned  it's  hard physically  mentally  financially  sometimes i do cry at night it's  definitely stressful knowing that you  know if if we can't do breast milk that  there might not be  an option for her it's like a scavenger  hunt every week we'll literally waste a  whole tank of gas in a day  trying to find formula and maybe only  find one can you show up at the parking  lot you've kind of got this hope and you  turn the corner uh into into the baby  food and formula aisle and it's just  absolutely barren and it you know it  breaks your heart a little bit more  every time it's crazy  it was like the toilet paper in the  sanitizer when everybody was looking for  a toilet paper and sanitizer gone off  the shelves he needs formula  i just don't understand why it's taking  so long when my daughter doesn't drink  the whole bottle i'm like oh my gosh she  just wasted like two three ounces i'm  like that was like a scoop that we could  have saved  [Music]  boy they don't call it liquid gold for  nothing right now american academy  pediatrics spokesperson and pediatrician  dr alana levine joins us now with more  on this i want to talk about what  parents can do if they have no formula  at all but let's talk about the other  situation first which is you go to the  store and the formula that you usually  have is not there so you have to make a  switch how how much can you switch and  what are the risks of doing so right so  we're actually in a crisis situation  right so the advice during a crisis  situation is different than under normal  circumstances so parents really need to  find an fda approved formula  that is appropriate for their child so  if you have a child that doesn't have  food allergies or special health needs  buy the formula that you can find on the  shelves and if that means you have to  switch every couple days  i'm okay with that i think we really  have to balance adequate nutrition with  safety right now if you have a  particular formula that your kids are  really uh stuck uh on uh and you're  running out you can't find replacements  is it okay to dilute the formula in the  short term in the hopes that you'll find  stuff in the medium term i'm gonna give  you a hard no on that it is really  dangerous to dilute formula you  impact the nutrition right so you're  diluting the actual essential nutrition  nutrients that the baby's getting and  that can lead to growth and development  problems in addition to which you're  totally throwing off the electrolyte  imbalance and those electrolytes are  critical and can lead to major health  problems like seizures for example do  you believe that we're even in this  situation yeah i mean as a doctor can  you even believe that in the united  states of america in 2022 you're sitting  here having this conversation it is  unfathomable to me that we are actually  here i i just i never in my 20-plus  years of practice ever thought we'd  arrive here but now were you gonna ask  this about parents making their own  formula because now some parents are  saying you know what i'll just make my  own go online and get a recipe right and  you know we used to do that generations  and generations ago but again i'm going  to caution parents against doing that  why because formula's so complex it's  this delicate balance of nutrients and  minerals the balance between protein and  fat and if you throw that off it could  actually be dangerous to the baby in  addition to which 